Agile Methodologies – Summary Content
1. What is Agile Methodology?

Agile methodology is a software development approach that focuses on flexibility, teamwork, customer satisfaction, and continuous improvement. It divides a project into small parts called iterations or sprints.
2. Principles of Agile

    Customer satisfaction through continuous delivery.

    Welcome changing requirements.

    Deliver working software frequently.

    Encourage teamwork and communication.

    Focus on simplicity.

    Continuous improvement and feedback.

3. Types of Agile Methodologies

    Scrum: Divides work into short periods called sprints (usually 1–4 weeks).

    Kanban: Uses a visual board to manage and track tasks.

    Extreme Programming (XP): Focuses on software quality, testing, and frequent releases.

    Lean: Reduces waste and improves efficiency.

    Crystal: Focuses on communication and teamwork.

    Feature-Driven Development (FDD): Develops software based on specific features.

4. Advantages of Agile

    Faster software delivery.

    Better customer satisfaction.

    Easy to handle changes.

    Improved teamwork.

    Continuous testing and feedback.

    Better product quality.

5. Disadvantages of Agile

    Requires continuous customer involvement.

    Difficult to predict total project cost and time.

    Needs experienced team members.

    Frequent changes can affect project planning.

    Less documentation in some Agile projects.

